WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.120
In 2014, some British YouTube stars were called
out by the British advertising regulators

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.890
for not properly declaring sponsored content.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.990
They mentioned the sponsorship in the description,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.440
they said "thanks to the company for making
this possible" at the end:

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.280
it wasn't like they were trying to hide it,
but it wasn't enough.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.240
It had to be clear before you clicked on the
video, that it was an advert.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.240
In a world so saturated with advertising:
why was that a problem?

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.810
Well, British media has some of the strongest
product placement laws in the world.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.750
Most of the rest of the planet, though?

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.050
They don't really care. And the reason,

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.780
as with so many things, goes back to history.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.859
Back in the first years of television,

00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:48.030
all Britain had was the BBC from Alexandra
Palace. Paid for not by advertising,

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.329
but by a yearly tax on anyone who owned a
television. And that's still true.

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:52.640
So advertising wasn't, and isn't,

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.190
allowed on the BBC. In the United States,
though,

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.469
they had commercial television right from
the start -- and a -- whaa! --

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:02.469
a show's host would regularly interrupt their
patter

00:01:02.469 --> 00:01:06.610
to give a personal endorsement down the camera
for a better brand of umbrella(!)

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.370
"Unless you're smoking L&amp;Ms, all you can do
is imagine how good they taste."

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.430
But in the UK, that sort of thing was seen
as horribly tacky.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.950
When commercial TV launched here,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.610
admags, weird half-soap-opera,

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.120
half-infomercials, existed for a few years,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.120
but it just wasn't the British way of doing
things, darling(!)

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.190
So in the 60s, strict rules were laid down:
advertising and

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:33.490
content must never collide. No product placement,
at all.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.440
Which led to some things that Americans would
find very strange:

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.840
our version of the Price is Right,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.460
for example, couldn't mention brand names.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.850
You might win a brand new car,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.370
but the announcer would never say what make
of car it was.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:49.090
"A 1.3 hatchback!"

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.770
Teleshopping and infomercials existed, and
still do,

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.430
but they had to be clearly flagged,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.830
on screen with a title, saying what they were.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.140
Were there dodgy dealings going on behind
the scenes?

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.360
Did some producers get backhanders for making
sure

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.700
that the heroes of their drama used a certain
type of computer?

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.540
Almost certainly. But you wouldn't see, for
example,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.670
those heroes stopping for lunch at one particular
chain restaurant

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.749
and emphasising how good the food was.

00:02:11.749 --> 00:02:13.900
"This Subway sandwich? So ono."

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.120
You know how the American Idol judges all
had those big branded cups in front of them?

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.129
They had to be blurred out when that series
was shown over here.

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:22.900
Other product placements, like those in movies,

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.200
or sports broadcasts coming from sponsored
arenas, were deemed acceptable,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.659
but the regulators generally took a hard line
on entertainment made here in Britain.

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:34.530
That's what the folks who grew up in Britain
are used to.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:38.709
That's what the people making the decisions
see as the standard for the country's television.

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:40.230
So as you might imagine,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.400
our advertising rules in general are a bit
more restrictive too.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:48.749
There is a thing called the Advertising Standards
Authority who write the Advertising Codes.

00:02:48.749 --> 00:02:51.790
Now, the codes are technically voluntary,

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.469
but in practice it's as good as the law.

00:02:53.469 --> 00:02:57.090
Because few companies want the bad publicity
that'll come from breaking it,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.260
no TV channels or newspapers will dare to
run ads that break the Codes,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.159
and ad agencies, the people who make the commercials,

00:03:04.159 --> 00:03:05.980
don't want to get in trouble either.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.629
Because the alternative is the government
starts setting the rules

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:12.019
instead of the advertising industry itself,

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:15.159
and they'll make breaking the Code an actual
criminal offence

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:17.700
rather than just something that gets your
advert pulled.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.569
Now, the rules have been relaxed recently.

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:24.109
Provided it's flagged up with a tiny symbol
at the start of the show,

00:03:24.109 --> 00:03:27.790
some commercial TV can now include product
placements.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.889
But for advertisers used to the permissiveness
of America,

00:03:30.889 --> 00:03:34.049
modern-day British TV is still a shock:

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:37.079
adverts can't editorially affect the show,
and

00:03:37.079 --> 00:03:40.129
they can't talk about how good the product
is. Also,

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:44.159
you can't product-place food that's high in
sugar, fat or salt,

00:03:44.159 --> 00:03:48.480
and there's absolutely no product placement
at all within children's programs.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.389
The Advertising Standards Authority lives
in the building behind me,

00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:55.590
and the Code they write has hundreds of pages
of rules governing all sorts of ads.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:58.959
But there are two very important sections
for what we're talking about,

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:00.769
and given the rules I mentioned earlier,

00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:02.469
they probably won't surprise you.

00:04:02.469 --> 00:04:04.939
Number 1: no matter where they are,

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:07.849
adverts must be clearly identifiable as adverts.

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:12.629
And number 2: you can't advertise unhealthy
food to children.

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:17.819
So you might see why the regulators got a
bit annoyed at vloggers with teen audiences

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:22.669
selling cookies in the middle of their regular
videos without any warning.

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:24.060
Now, to be fair:

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.379
the regulators hadn't actually produced any
help or advice for the industry on this

00:04:28.379 --> 00:04:30.879
back then, a year and a half ago as I record
this.

00:04:30.879 --> 00:04:31.940
They were caught on the back foot.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.580
And the vloggers themselves weren't really
expected to know this,

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.979
it was the ad agency's job -- the client that
hired them --

00:04:38.979 --> 00:04:40.530
to check that they were compliant.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:45.190
And the agencies either got carried away at
what seemed like this new, unregulated world,

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.530
or they were American and didn't know or care
about it.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:54.069
British TV is still seen, at least by people
in Britain, as a gold standard.

00:04:54.069 --> 00:04:56.419
We don't do those horrible product placements.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:04:59.360
Not on television, anyway. As for YouTube,

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.250
and the rest of the internet: well,

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:02.990
the rules are still being written.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.550
All of which is a long-winded way of saying
that

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.270
my videos are open for product placement now,

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:07.949
if you're a company: do get in touch!

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.440
